Updated `src/blog/people/drafts/Nathan-Fielder.md`.

## What I changed

**Two publication-blocking attribution errors, both confirmed against sources rather than taken on the reviewers' word:**

1. **The three-day memorization protocol was Jack Black's, not Fielder's.** Two independent audits place it at 14:11–16:46 of the Black Q&A, with Fielder asking and Black answering. It was carrying four things: a ledger row, the ledger section's climax, a persona-section beat, and the Rabbit Hole's Type 1 rebuttal. All four are gone. The Type 1 counter is rebuilt on set-detail perfectionism (rightness-held-unwitnessed vs. believability-to-a-room), which is sourced.

2. **"You've ruined the whole thing for me" is performed dialogue.** I fetched the Variety review directly: an actor Fielder hired to play the participant delivers the premiere's verdict, and Variety ran a correction on exactly this. The draft had presented it as the emotional centerpiece and doubled down ("That is not a critic's inference"). Replaced with the true version, which is better — the harshest line about Fielder in the episode is one he commissioned, staged and edited in.

**The replacement climax is Dumb Starbucks**, which fresh eyes correctly flagged as absent. It pre-armored against the fight he expected (a fair-use FAQ taped inside for Starbucks' lawyers) and was closed by the LA County Health Department over a permit he hadn't thought to get. That is a stronger version of the article's own argument than what it replaced.

**Other corrections:** un-ranked the pilot motives (the source gives both together); Emma Stone's role (she stars opposite him and has been directed by him, not a co-director); dropped the invented sidewalk inner monologue that was carrying Safdie's later observation; removed "unlimited access," "paying audience," the invented "ten percent," the "four years" at UVic, the unasserted Humber year, and a Type 3 counter-test built on a stereotype. The unverified caption fragment "I felt insecure" is removed rather than carried forward — the transcript notes never sanctioned it.

**Rewrote the close on real reporting:** Holmes invited the crew 34 days before prison, Fielder moved into the San Diego house, Emma Stone moderated the Telluride Q&A, A24 releases October 16. His line there — that he's struggling to understand what he experienced and wants the audience to help him — is the twenty dollars again, at scale.

Also moved the persona H2 from slot 5 to 3, expanded it with the harder read (the preparation gap *is* the awkwardness, not a layer under it), added ~200 words of plain-language description of the shows, and connected Summit Ice to Vancouver Talmud Torah without asserting causation.

## Verification after the pass

- **Source audit:** 2 load-bearing quotes, 2 inline, 0 vague, 0 untagged.
- **blog-lint:** 0 fail, 1 warn (missing frontmatter `faqs`, owned by the enrich stage).
- **Body: 3,900 words**, top of the 3,200–3,900 band. Ledger quotes were cut to short slices to bring four over-budget sources under the 25-word cap.
- **Same-type similarity:** clear, top pair down from 0.129 to 0.066.

## Rejected

Fresh eyes suggested folding the pilot narrative into the ledger table. That would delete the piece's only critic-pressure anchor (FAA, Rep. Cohen, Goglia's partial support), so I removed the duplicated table row instead and kept the H2.

## Still open

The three Q&A sources remain unlabeled auto-captions. Everything drawn from them is now paraphrase and no caption-derived quotation survives, but **the audio listening check has still never been performed** — that's what let another person's biography into the draft in the first place. Any future pass wanting verbatim quotation from those Q&As has to do that check first. The Hollywood Reporter (2024) and Rolling Stone (2025) interviews still return 402 and are unread.

Given the scale of what the audits caught, this is worth an editor pass before it goes near `--publish`.
